  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Solve for $y$ where $\\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{4y}=\\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $4y,\\,{y}^{2},$ and $2{y}^{2}.$ First, we find the denominators' least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$). We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(4y,{y}^{2},2{y}^{2})=4{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4{y}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{4y} & =\\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4{y}^{2}(\\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{5}{4y}) & =4{y}^{2}(\\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 4{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-4{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4y} & =4{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y+2}{{y}^{2}}-4y\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{5}{4y} & =2\\cdot 2{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\cdot \\cancel{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{y+2}{\\cancel{{y}^{2}}}-\\cancel{4y}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{4y}} & =2\\cdot \\cancel{2{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\cancel{2{y}^{2}}} \\\\ 4(y+2)-y\\cdot 5 & =2\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $y,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(y+2)-y\\cdot 5 & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\ 4y+8-5y & =6 \\\\ -y+8 & =6 \\\\ -y & =-2 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $y=0$ as a solution. But since $y=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $y=2.$",
